	myHeader "branch = \"DLC2\", contextComment = \"In World Message Title\", enabled = true, guid = #91D5D542679CE7EE000000706AE78E31, lineVersion = 3, maxLength = 0, productId = -1, sectionId = 12368, text = \"Expedition Journal\", type = \"MenuLine\""
	myDescription "branch = \"DLC2\", contextComment = \"In World Message Description\", enabled = true, guid = #91D5D542679CE7EE00000071A3E3F1DC, lineVersion = 4, maxLength = 0, productId = -1, sectionId = 12368, text = \"Journal of Professor Gil Orum, Imperial Science Academy\n\nKhepi Expedition, day ten. On the surface, the Sayma Nebula is a standard Type 15A stellological phenomenon, similar to the Maelstrom Nebula or any of the larger formations in the Trax sector. But the mysteries beneath are unlike anything I've seen in all my years of surveying.\n\nAfter employing the locals to rig up some improvised magnetoshields, we were able to penetrate the outer layer with our nav systems intact. What we saw beneath the clouds was thoroughly unexpected: another ship, drifting through space and unresponsive to our communications. When we approached, anxious the safety of whatever crew was aboard, it suddenly vanished. I might have questioned my sanity, had not all of us witnessed the same thing.\n\nThe Khepi system has fierce defenses of a nature I cannot quite determine. It puts me in mind of a sentient creature slashing at the interloper that dared to enter its lair. I try to keep a rational mind, but I cannot help the ominous feeling of trespassing. Of going somewhere I am not welcome.\n\nIn any case, it will make for a thrilling holomonograph.\", type = \"MenuLine\"}"
}

